{"type":"text","timestamp":1792266936259,"sessionID":"ses_eb497bd88ffeX3pVcjeXh7k0AQ","part":{"id":"prt_14b6edc93001QHpBDA61I1Ee1Y","messageID":"msg_14b6c722c00172ClYt57Ib6T2N","sessionID":"ses_eb497bd88ffeX3pVcjeXh7k0AQ","type":"text","text":"All sources have been fetched and verified (including the Goedel-Prover repo, the FormalAlign full text, and the HTML rendering of the 2605.22763 paper, since its PDF URL returns raw PDF bytes). Here is the assessment:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"items\": [\n    {\n      \"ref\": \"p1:c1\",\n      \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"page_quote\": \"Training completed in under 24 hours using 8 H100 GPUs. Each problem receives 16 total formalizations (8 from each formalizer). ... Data Scale: 928K informal statements processed; 1.64M formalized statements; 140K Leanworkbook statements; Total: 1.78M formal statements.\",\n      \"note\": \"Recorded quote appears verbatim on the project page, and every number in the item matches the page: 928K informal statements, 1.64M formalized statements, 1.78M total including 140K Lean Workbook statements, two formalizers (A and B) each producing 8 candidates per problem, under 24 hours on 8 H100 GPUs.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ref\": \"p1:c2\",\n      \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"page_quote\": \"FC Test: Using LLM as judge to verify accuracy and completeness. ... Uses Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct for assessment; Generates 4 independent judgments per statement; Statements below 0.5 FC score are filtered out.\",\n      \"note\": \"All three quote fragments appear verbatim under the Faithfulness and Completeness (FC) Test. The page shows the semantic gate is the external Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct LLM judge (the Lean compiler is used only for syntax in the CC test); no human or probe-based semantic check is described.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ref\": \"p1:c3\",\n      \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"page_quote\": \"We train statement formalizers to translate the natural language math problems from Numina into formal language (Lean 4), creating a dataset of 1.64 million formal statements. LLMs are used to check that the formal statements accurately preserve the content of the original natural language problems.\",\n      \"note\": \"The recorded quote appears verbatim in the v2 abstract, tied directly to the 1.64M dataset; the introduction further says they 'employ LLMs to ensure that the formal statements preserve the content of the informal statements', supporting the item's framing (the paper says check/ensure rather than 'guarantee', but presents the LLM check as the preservation mechanism for the 1.64M statements).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ref\": \"p1:done\",\n      \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"page_quote\": \"CC Test: Using LEAN compiler to verify syntax; FC Test: Using LLM as judge to verify accuracy and completeness.\",\n      \"note\": \"Recorded quote appears verbatim. I checked all three artifacts the item references: the paper (arXiv 2502.07640v2), the project page, and the GitHub repo README — all describe only the CC (Lean compiler) and FC (LLM-as-judge) tests; none describes hidden-state probes on the formalizer or any machine-labelled vs expert-labelled comparison for misformalization detection.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ref\": \"p2:c1\",\n      \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"page_quote\": \"We then employ a QwQ-32B as a judge to evaluate the semantic correctness of each remaining formalization, using a handcrafted prompt to guide its assessment. ... We observe that using multiple samples from the judge model and unanimous voting structure significantly reduces the amount of false positives with little impact on true positives.\",\n      \"note\": \"Quote appears verbatim in Appendix C.2.2. The judge is the external QwQ-32B (the autoformalizer itself is a fine-tuned Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct), and the page confirms multiple samples with unanimous voting.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ref\": \"p2:c2\",\n      \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"page_quote\": \"Since the reward signal in this task is inherently fuzzy, and LLM-based feedback is susceptible to false positives, we monitor model outputs throughout training with Lean 4 experts and gradually improve the judge prompt over iterations.\",\n      \"note\": \"The recorded quote fragment appears verbatim; the page's stated reason (LLM feedback susceptible to false positives, i.e. the judge misses real errors) matches the item's 'because the judge misses real errors'.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ref\": \"p2:c3\",\n      \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"page_quote\": \"We note that this method required careful expert monitoring as we observe that the model would repeatedly make mistakes that the LLM judge model cannot catch, as long as it passed compilation. ... we use a human-curated test set of size approximately 1,000 ... our annotated statements are limited in number—approximately 10k—compared to the 100k generated by autoformalization ... we established a dedicated annotation team tasked with reviewing and refining the outputs from the autoformalization process.\",\n      \"note\": \"Every sub-claim verified verbatim on the page: the residual-gap quote (C.2.3), the ~1,000 human-curated test set, the dedicated annotation team, and the ~10k human-refined statements vs ~100k autoformalized ones (C.3).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ref\": \"p2:done\",\n      \"status\": \"PARTIAL\",\n      \"page_quote\": \"Evaluated across four benchmarks augmented by our proposed misalignment strategies, FormalAlign demonstrates superior performance. ... We fine-tune a Mistral-7B model as the FormalAlign model ... FormalAlign combines the cross-entropy loss in sequence autoformalization and the contrastive loss in hidden states to enhance the informal-formal alignment.\",\n      \"note\": \"Recorded quote appears on the cited abstract page, and the full text confirms the substance: a separately fine-tuned Mistral-7B evaluator with hidden-state-based contrastive alignment scoring, whose misaligned examples are synthetic (generated via the proposed misalignment strategies/random pairing — no probes on a production formalizer, no expert-found real errors). The Kimina paper confirms Zhengying Liu is a Kimi-team core contributor and that Kimina's loop uses the QwQ-32B judge plus Lean 4 experts. However, 'ICLR 2025' is stated nowhere on the abstract page or the fetched preprint, and Jing Xiong is not listed among the Numina/Kimi contributors on the Kimina paper (only Zhengying Liu is; Jianqiao Lu is a Numina contributor), so those two sub-claims are unverified on the fetched pages.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ref\": \"p3:c1\",\n      \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"page_quote\": \"We established a bridge between these two complementary spheres by fine-tuning a Gemini model to automatically translate natural language problem statements into formal statements, creating a large library of formal problems of varying difficulty.\",\n      \"note\": \"Quote appears verbatim on the blog; it further states 'We trained AlphaProof for the IMO by proving or disproving millions of problems', confirming the Gemini-translated formal library as AlphaProof's training data.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ref\": \"p3:c2\",\n      \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"page_quote\": \"Process infographic of AlphaProof's reinforcement learning training loop: Around one million informal math problems are translated into a formal math language by a formalizer network. Then a solver network searches for proofs or disproofs of the problems...\",\n      \"note\": \"The recorded quote appears verbatim on the blog as the training-loop infographic caption, stating the ~1M scale translated by the formalizer network.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ref\": \"p3:c3\",\n      \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"page_quote\": \"First, the problems were manually translated into formal mathematical language for our systems to understand.\",\n      \"note\": \"Quote appears verbatim in the blog's account of the IMO 2024 run, confirming manual human translation of the contest problems; 'did not trust the autoformalizer at all' is the card's gloss of this stated fact.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ref\": \"p3:c4\",\n      \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"page_quote\": \"We used Gemini to autoformalize 492 open questions from the OEIS and applied the agent to the resulting Lean statements. ... Our most capable agent autonomously resolved 9 of 353 open Erdős problems at the per-problem cost of a few hundred dollars, proved 44/492 OEIS conjectures ... AlphaProof cost approximately 27.5 TPU hours ($60 USD) per problem on v6e TPUs.\",\n      \"note\": \"Quote with 492 appears verbatim in the DeepMind research-level follow-up paper; substantial per-problem compute is stated explicitly (a few hundred dollars per problem; ~27.5 TPU-hours for AlphaProof per problem). The paper itself acknowledges the misformalization risk ('As a guard against misformalization, the agent was required to prove test lemmas...'), grounding the item's rationale. Verified via the paper's HTML rendering, since the named PDF URL returns raw PDF bytes (metadata confirms the same paper).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ref\": \"p3:done\",\n      \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"page_quote\": \"First, the problems were manually translated into formal mathematical language for our systems to understand. ... We established a bridge between these two complementary spheres by fine-tuning a Gemini model to automatically translate natural language problem statements into formal statements, creating a large library of formal problems of varying difficulty.\",\n      \"note\": \"Recorded quote appears verbatim. The blog describes only the fine-tuned Gemini formalizer (for the training library) plus manual translation for the contest; neither it nor the fetched DeepMind follow-up paper (2605.22763v1) reports any probe-based misformalization detection or machine-vs-expert label experiment. The parenthetical note (formalizer is closed) is consistent with the page's fine-tuned proprietary Gemini model.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```","time":{"start":1792266919059,"end":1792266936232}}}
